{"id": "190118", "revid": "2236161", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=190118", "title": "Afuze", "text": "Afuze is in Edo State in South South Nigeria.\nUnderstand.\nAfuze is the name of a town and the administrative headquarters of Owan East Local Government Area.\nGeography.\nAfuze is positioned in the northern part of Edo State. The town's landscape is characterized by a combination of undulating terrain and regional vegetation.\nClimate.\nAfuze experiences a wet season that is warm, humid, and often cloudy, while the dry season is characterized by hot, muggy conditions with periods of partial sunshine. Throughout the year, temperatures generally range between 66\u00b0F and 90\u00b0F, seldom dropping below 60\u00b0F or rising above 95\u00b0F.\nConsidering the weather conditions, the most suitable time to visit Afuze for hot-weather and outdoor activities is from mid-November to early February.\nConnect.\nYou would most likely find a reliable coverage of the 2G/3G mobile networks in most locations in Afuze. Signal strength of 4G is relatively inadequate in the town, and you are less likely to experience network outage for any of the mobile phone service providers in any of the area. Bear in mind that different networks tend to be more stable and reliable in different locations of the city at different times, though all will almost perform satisfactory well in any location."}
{"id": "190119", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=190119", "title": "Fugar", "text": "Fugar is in South South Nigeria.\nUnderstand.\nFugar is a town and Local Government Area in Edo State, Nigeria.\nConnect.\nYou would most likely find a reliable coverage of the 2G/3G mobile networks in most locations in Fugar. The signal strength of 4G is relatively inadequate in the town, and you are less likely to experience network outage for any of the mobile phone service providers in any of the areas east. Bear in mind that different networks tend to be more stable and reliable in different locations of the city at different times, though all will almost perform satisfactory well in any location.\nMTN, 9Mobile, Airtel (Zain) and GLO are all available service providers. So if you are planning a visit to Edo State, you might not have to be so much concerned about the service provider to choose from as they all are reasonably reliable.\nIt's worthy to note when visiting villages and rural areas in most parts of the state Glo offers a more reliable presence in villages than others."}
{"id": "190120", "revid": "1342202", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=190120", "title": "Ibillo", "text": "Ibillo is in Edo State, South South Nigeria.\nUnderstand.\nIbillo is one of the largest towns in the Akoko-Edo local government area.\nConnect.\nYou would most likely find a reliable coverage of the 2G/3G mobile networks in most locations in Ibillo. The signal strength of 4G is relatively inadequate in the town, and you are less likely to experience network outage for any of the mobile phone service providers in any of the areas east. Bear in mind that different networks tend to be more stable and reliable in different locations of the city at different times, though all will almost perform satisfactory well in any location."}
{"id": "190121", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=190121", "title": "Ode Remo", "text": "Ode Remo is a town in Ogun State.\nUnderstand.\nOde Remo's people are Yorubas from Ile-Ife, the Yorubas' cradle. They originated in the ancient city of Ile-Ilode Ife's and Iremo neighborhoods. They travelled through dense woodland, rivers, and streams until they arrived at a site on the Ijebu Waterside where they attempted to settle; this location is now known as \"Ode Omi.\" However, the oracle, who served as a guide, advised them to continue on until they arrived at the current location where they settled. After migrating from the Ile-Ife quarters of Ilode and Iremo, the two names were used interchangeably until Ode Remo became the permanent name of this historical town.\nGet in.\nOde Remo is easily accessible from Lagos and Ibadan due to its location on the Lagos-Ibadan Expressway. The majority of visitors arrive by shuttle from Lagos/Ibadan on the main road. Motorcycles are the most common mode of transportation in Ode-Remo, with prices as low as \u20a6100 (Dec 2021) for short distances.\nEat.\nRiki mart food and drinks "}
{"id": "190122", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=190122", "title": "Ethiope", "text": "The River Ethiope flows through Nigeria's Delta State. The river begins in Umuaja and passes through a number of communities before reaching the sea at Sapele. This article covers part of Delta State east of Orerokpe.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe closest airport to Ethiope is Benin City Airport (BNI), which is about 90 km away. Other nearby airports are: Port Harcourt (PHC). It is about 145 km from Owerri (QOW). Enugu (ENU), about 161 km away; Lagos (LOS), about 227 km away; about 293 km away. Air transportation to the city is via the Osubi Runway (also known as Wari Airport) in the nearby town of Osubi. Agbolikoko also has a small runway that served as the only airport until the construction of a large airport in Osbi. Arik Air and Aero Contractors are some of the major airlines operating commercial flights on this route.\nBy road.\nRoad transport is the most widely used mode of transport nationwide, especially in international and domestic transport. \nGet around.\nThe main modes of transportation are public buses and tricycles (commonly known as Keke Napep). There is also a jetty to move through the waterways.\nSee.\nThe River Ethiope is an exceptionally beautiful river. A wide variety of animals and plants inhabit. The river provides ecosystem services such as flood protection, climate regulation, recreation, spiritual tribute, water supply, food, research and education, medicine, and building materials to the many communities in which it flows. The Ethiopian River's capacity, humans and other organisms that provide these ecosystem services, continue to provide, but are seriously hampered by development pressures and the consequences of climate change due to poverty, ignorance and corruption.\nThe River Ethiope, considered to be the deepest inland waterway in Africa, is formed in contrast to other rivers at the foot of a giant silk cotton tree in Ummaja, Ukuwani City, Delta, and flows into Sapelet. increase. Abraca, it has become like a cave enough to accommodate ocean-going vessels. Its appeal is its uniqueness and unnatural purity. Fish swim enthusiastically in the water, but as soon as they feel the presence of humans, they go out of reach. The river is now home to many attractions and is said to help irrigate 60,000 hectares of land in Abraca for the initiative to develop high quality cassava flour for federal cassava bread and mechanized cassava farms. Tourists visiting the river source should, at the discretion of the high priest, cut off from either \u20a61,500 or \u20a62,000 to see the river source."}
{"id": "190130", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=190130", "title": "Okpekpe", "text": "Okpekpe is in South South Nigeria.\nUnderstand.\nOkpekpe Township is situated in Etsako East Local Government Area of Edo State, Nigeria. It is located about 25 km north east of Auchi.\nClimate.\nOkpekpe is influenced by two climatic conditions namely; dry and wet seasons. The wet season starts from May to October, while the dry season starts from November to April.\nConnect.\nYou would most likely find a reliable coverage of the 2G/3G mobile networks in most locations in Okpekpe. The signal strength of 4G is relatively inadequate in the town, and you are less likely to experience network outage for any of the mobile phone service providers in any of the areas east. Bear in mind that different networks tend to be more stable and reliable in different locations of the city at different times, though all will almost perform satisfactory well in any location.\nMTN, 9Mobile, Airtel (Zain) and GLO are all available service providers. So if you are planning a visit to Edo State, you might not have to be so much concerned about the service provider to choose from as they all are reasonably reliable.\nIn villages and rural areas in most parts of the state, Glo offers a more reliable presence in villages than others."}
{"id": "190135", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=190135", "title": "Weslaco (Texas)", "text": "Weslaco is a town in the Rio Grande Valley along the border between Texas and Tamaulipas, Mexico. It is a popular area for birdwatching.\nUnderstand.\nWeslaco is one of many cities in the Valley, particularly along I-2, where they seem to line up like an interminable chain of pearls. Some curiosities that set Weslaco apart are that its name was derived from a company, the W. E. Stewart Land Company, that had purchased the area from another, and that it has numerous parks and reserves devoted to nature preservation and study, creating somewhat of an incongruous departure from the agriculture and development that the casual tourist first appreciates in the Valley.\nGet in.\nAs Interstate 2 passes through the municipality, you can easily get here from McAllen to the west or Brownsville areas to the east, using your own vehicle or modalities discussed on the main Rio Grande Valley page, particularly Valley Metro[dead link].\nBy bus.\nValley Transit[dead link] provides bus service between cities in the Rio Grande Valley with connections to Greyhound and several of the trans-border bus lines that operate in South Texas.\nDo.\nThe bulk of attractions in Weslaco are ironically natural in origin. Just when it seems that nature would be nonexistent in this sea of fields and houses, opportunities avail."}
{"id": "190137", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=190137", "title": "Tarascon", "text": "Tarascon is a town on the eastern bank of the Rh\u00f4ne in the Provence in the southeast of France.\nUnderstand.\nThe city was founded in 48 by the Romans. The town's history is shaped by \"The Legend of Martha and Tarasque\". According to population legend, the dragon Tarasque devoured travellers on the banks of the Rh\u00f4ne and was banished by Saint Martha. This legend also gave the city its current name; its previous name is said to have been Nerluc.\nGet in.\nTarascon sits halfway between Arles and Avignon, around 20 km from each. \nBy train.\nThe nearest TGV stations are Gare d'Avignon TGV, Arles, and Nimes.\nBy bus.\nThere are several bus lines from towns all over the Bouches-du-Rh\u00f4ne, Gard and Vaucluse. You can always buy your passage from the conductor.\nBy boat.\nWhile there is no regular boat line in Tarascon, you can arrive by boat in Tarascon via river cruises.\nGet around.\nIn the old town, within the old city walls, there are only a few parking possibilities. However, there are parking spaces on the edge of the old town, from which it can be reached in just a few minutes.\nThe bus network in and around Tarascon is maintained by Envia.\nTarascon is for the most part small enough to enjoy by foot.\nSee.\nTown centre:\nBeyond the town centre:\nSleep.\nCamping Saint Gabriel, Route de Fontvieille in St Etienne du Gres. Tel.: +33 4 90 91 19 83, email: contact@campingsaintgabriel.com. Open: March-November.\nCamping Municipal Alpilles Provence, Avenue du Doctor Barberin, 13103 Saint Etienne du Gres. Tel: +33 4 90 49 00 03."}
{"id": "190138", "revid": "113846", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=190138", "title": "Ibokun", "text": "Ibokun (also known as IIemure, Ilu Obokun, Ilu'bokun) is a town in Osun State, South West, Nigeria. Ibokun is the headquarters of Obokun. \nUnderstand.\nIbokun (formerly known as Ilemure) was called for the warrior who defended the town against attacks by neighbouring fighters, including Alare, the Ilare monarch. Obokun, the youngest son of Oduduwa, was thought to have traveled from If\u1eb9. In order to treat his father's blindness, he offered to get sea water, which is known as Obokun in Yoruba.\nClimate.\nIn Ibokun, the dry season is hot, humid, and partially cloudy, while the wet season is warm, stuffy, and overcast. The average annual temperature is 64-91 F; it is seldom lower than 58 F or higher than 96 F. Early November to mid-February is the ideal time of year to visit Ibokun for hot-weather activities, according to the beach/pool score. The average daily maximum temperature during the 2-month hot season, which runs from January 23 to March 24, is above 89 F. March is the hottest month of the year in Ibokun. The 3.8-month cool season runs from June 15 to October 10. August is the coolest month of the year in Ibokun.\nGet around.\nBy car.\nPublic vehicles usually found at motorparks are available at a slightly higher fare. This option lowers the wait time in waiting for vehicle to be full and also time of arrival as it is faster.\nConnect.\nAll major mobile phone service networks are available in Ibokun but with varying degree of reliability and usability. Often times, it is customary to experience network outage for all or some of the mobile phone service providers. Such situation is particularly common in some remote areas of the city. And it is also common for different networks to be more stable and reliable in different locations of the city at different times.\nIt is preferable if you are planning a visit to this city to have multiple SIM cards that cut across all the mobile service providers as you can't tell which will be more stable and reliable at a time in your location during your visit. And if you will risk not having any of them, you might be better off not risking the MTN network, because,from indications, its seems to perform well than the others, though its rates may be high and its offers and services might be stringent, but you might be more assured of seamless voice and data connection. While 4G network might not be widespread in city, you could be assured of a fairly stable 2G and 3Gs broadband cellular networks.\nGlo (Globacom) boasts of fast network speed for internet browsing activities, but that might only be enjoyable in limited locations. Airtel is fairly satisfactory for all you might need to connect to the world, including rates and network stability. As for 9mobile, they also perform well in internet browsing speed but not without locational limitations."}
{"id": "190139", "revid": "2331762", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=190139", "title": "Esa Oke", "text": "Esa Oke is a Yoruba town in Osun State in South West Nigeria.\nUnderstand.\nEsa Oke is a native Ijesa (Ijesha) community. Omiran Adebolu, a member of the Olofin family of Ile-Ife, though disputably, was believed to have founded Esa-Oke after he left Ile-Ife in 1184 AD.\nThe town has at the head of its traditional governance structure, a kingship that goes with the title of the 'Owamiran of Esa-Oke'. The Ijesa North traditional Council of the Osun state Council of Obas is the umbrella body that oversees its affairs. There are four broad divisional heads reporting to the king representing: Oke-Esa, Erinjiyan, Idofin and Odo-Ese, each with a divisional chieftaincy title of: Asaba, Enurin, Odobaja and Asalu respectively.\nClimate.\nEsa-Oke has a tropical savanna (Aw) climate, typical of southwestern Nigeria. It is warm throughout the year with distinct wet and dry seasons.\nGet in.\nEsa-Oke is very accessible with good road network that connects to neighbouring towns and states, especially the Osun state capital, Osogbo, in about 1 hour 30 minutes.\nGet around.\nBy car.\nPublic vehicles usually found at motorparks are available at a slightly higher fare. This option lowers the wait time in waiting for vehicle to be full and also time of arrival as it is faster.\nSee.\nOsun State College of Technology.\nOsun State College of Technology (OSCOTECH) has faculties covering engineering, environmental studies, management, applied sciences, and communication and information technology. It is particularly associated with technical education, vocational training, entrepreneurship and technological development in the area.&lt;ref&gt;\nThe campus may be of interest to visitors exploring the educational institutions and modern development of Esa-Oke."}
{"id": "190141", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=190141", "title": "Potiskum", "text": "Potiskum is one of the largest cities of about (483,000 in 2022) in Yobe State, northeastern Nigeria.\nIt has one of the largest cattle markets in Africa and the largest in West Africa, and also a large grain and millet market.\nUnderstand.\nClimate.\nPotiskum has two seasons, rainy and dry seasons. Rainfall is generally seasonal, variable, as well as heavy in this part of the city and it occurs between the months of March and October through November. The wet season peaks in July, lasting more than 290 days. The only dry months are January and February having little to no effect.\nGet in.\nThere is no airport in Potiskum or Yobe State, however, there are airports nearby by which one can get in through:\nBy bus.\nThe most common way to get into the Potiskum is by bus. There are a lot of bus stations that offer services from any state in Nigeria to Yobe State, and you can then take a cab or bus into Potiskum. Some of these services are:\nand many others.\nGet around.\nGetting around in Potiskum is not difficult, as there are buses available to take you around, all you need to do is to board the bus in Potiskum, tell the driver the area you are going to and the driver will take you there, however, you need to inform the driver and keep reminding him/her, so they don't end up missing your bus stops.\nConnect.\nAll major mobile phone service networks are available in Potiskum but with varying degrees of reliability and usability. It is usual to occasionally experience network outages for all or some of the mobile phone service providers. Such a situation is particularly common in some remote areas of the city. And it is also common for different networks to be more stable and reliable in different locations of the city at different times.\nif you are planning a visit to this city, you should consider having multiple SIM cards preferably for all the mobile service providers, since you can't tell which will be more stable and reliable than other at a time in your location during your visit. And if you will risk not having any of them, you might be better off not risking the MTN network, because, from indications, it seems to perform well than the others, though its rates may be high and its offers and services might be stringent, you are less likely to be concerned of erratic voice and data connection compared to others. The various broadband cellular network available include 2G, 3G, and 4G networks. And unlike many other cities in the country, there is wider coverage of the 4G network in most locations in the city.\nOf the service providers, it is Glo (or Globacom), which boasts of fast network speed for internet browsing activities, but that might not be enjoyable in some locations in Potiskum. Airtel is fairly satisfactory for all you might need to connect to the world, including rates and network stability. And 9mobile usually also performs well in internet browsing speed but not without locational limitations."}
{"id": "190143", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=190143", "title": "Belfield", "text": "Belfield is a city located on the eastern edge of the North Dakota Badlands.\nUnderstand.\nBelfield (population 800) is a small city in Stark County, North Dakota and only 15 minutes from Theodore Roosevelt National Park. Belfield boasts hotels and restaurants and multiple city parks for residents and tourists to enjoy. Belfield was founded in 1883, and has seen many boom and busts in this oil rich part of North Dakota.\nGet in.\nBelfield is located at the intersection of Interstate 94 and US Highway 85. I-94 is a major interstate highway that runs east-west across the state, use exit 42.\nGet around.\nA private car is a necessity."}
{"id": "190145", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=190145", "title": "Lingao", "text": "Lingao (\u4e34\u9ad8; L\u00edng\u0101o) is a county in the northwest of Hainan, China.\nUnderstand.\nLingao is best known for Cape Lingao (\u4e34\u9ad8\u89d2; L\u00edng\u0101oji\u01ceo) - the site of a historic lighthouse and the main landing site for the People's Liberation Army (PLA) during the Hainan Campaign of the Chinese Civil War. The area around Cape Lingao is now known as Hainan Liberation Park.\nThe county seat of Lingao is the town of Lincheng (\u4e34\u57ce; L\u00ednch\u00e9ng). \nGet in.\nLingao may be reached by both bus and train. Some visitors prefer to take the bus because the train station is quite far (about 16 km by road) from Lincheng's town center whereas the Lingao Bus Station is in a much more central location. \nGet around.\nIn Lincheng, the public bus network extends from Lingao South Railway Station in the south to Hainan Liberation Park in the north. Taxis and auto-rickshaws are also available.\nLong-distance buses operate between the Lingao Bus Station in Lincheng and other towns in Lingao."}
{"id": "190150", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=190150", "title": "Kwali", "text": "Kwali is an area council in Federal Capital Territory of Nigeria, North Central Nigeria. One of the major occupation of its inhabitants is pottery. \nGet in.\nBy plane.\nFrom the airport, you are best advised to take the official Green Cab. Board the cab with only people you know. Uber operates here as well. The Abuja light rail system connects the airport with the city center. Bolt (formerly Taxify) also operates extensively within the city and off-rides to and fro the airport. The airport is quite a distance from the main city. There are also private shuttle services offered to customers in the city. \nSo once you get into Abuja through this route, then you can get into Kwali from the airport by using either the cabs or car transport services like Uber or Bolt \nBy bus.\nYou can get into Kwali by bus from major cities like Lagos, Benin, Kano and Port Harcourt. Reliable services are available to bring you into Kwali, such as: ABC Transport (with air-conditioned luxurious bus rides), Ekene Dili Chukwu, Chisco Transport (CTN), Young Shall Grow and God is good motors.\nIt is advisable to take day trip buses from wherever you are coming from in the country. This is because the interstate roads have most part of them unmaintained and full of potholes. And also the issue of being attacked by robbers by night. If you're unfamiliar with the country, endeavor to take a bus with an escort. Buses allow you to appreciate the terrain, the towns and cities, and the subtle changes in these and culture as you drive towards the capital either from the south or the north. \nAfter getting into Kwali, there are cabs around by which you can get around with.\nBy train.\nNigeria Railway Corporation has completed an inter-city line between Abuja and Kaduna, where you can connect with the rest of the Nigerian rail system. Trains arrive and depart from Idu Station in Abuja which is about 15 km west of the city centre. As of 2018, there are four daily departures in each direction. The train service offers three types of fares as Economy, First Class, and Sleeper. The schedules and current fairs can be found on the Nigeria Railway Corporation website. It is a very comfortable mode of travelling.\nConnect.\nKwali has reliable mobile network coverage from providers like MTN, Glo, and Airtel, making it easy to stay connected. You can access internet services via mobile data or at cybercaf\u00e9s in the area. While electricity is available, occasional outages might occur, so plan accordingly."}
{"id": "190151", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=190151", "title": "Bwari", "text": "Bwari is one of the six area councils in the Federal Capital Territory in North Central Nigeria. It is near Abuja.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nYou can get into Bwari by bus from major cities like Lagos, Benin, Kano and Port Harcourt. Reliable services are available to bring you into Bwari, such as: ABC Transport (with air-conditioned luxurious bus rides), Ekene Dili Chukwu, Chisco Transport (CTN), Young Shall Grow and God is good motors.\nIt is advisable to take day trip buses from wherever you are coming from in the country. This is because the interstate roads have most part of them unmaintained and full of potholes. And also the issue of being attacked by robbers by night. If you're unfamiliar with the country, endeavor to take a bus with an escort. Buses allow you to appreciate the terrain, the towns and cities, and the subtle changes in these and culture as you drive towards the capital either from the south or the north. \nAfter getting into Bwari, there are cabs around by which you can get around with."}
{"id": "190152", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=190152", "title": "Abiriba", "text": "Abiriba is in Abia State in South East Nigeria.\nUnderstand.\nAbiriba regarded as an ancient Enuda kingdom. It is located in the Ohafia local government area in the state.\nAt the heart of Abiriba \u2014 the very centre of the town \u2014 is a large, ancient tree \"Okpu Achi\" (Achi tree). The tree is both symbolic and spiritual; and many believe that in the event of its fall, water from Okpu Achi would engulf the entire Abiriba land. Legend also has it that the Ishimokoto River is so mystical that when foreigners tried to leave town with a bottle of water from the river \u2014 the bottle broke apart and the water flew miles back to the river.\nClimate.\nAbiriba, located in Nigeria's Abia State, experiences a tropical climate, characterized by consistently hot temperatures year-round (often 90s\u00b0F/30s\u00b0C), high humidity, and distinct rainy and dry seasons typical of West Africa, with periods of sunny, clear weather punctuated by potential cloudiness and occasional showers, requiring sunscreen and awareness of pests. \nSeasons: The area experiences a typical tropical climate, characterized by a distinct rainy (wet) season and a drier dry season, which is common across the region.\nDrink.\nThe drinks available in Abiriba include native palm wine, and all brands of the beers in Nigeria, including wines and spirits\nConnect.\nNetwork coverage in Abiriba town range from 2G/3G mobile networks. Signal strength for 4G might diminish further away from the city centre, and you might occasionally experience network outages for all or some of the mobile phone service providers in the remote areas of the town. Different networks tend to be more stable and reliable in different locations of the town at different times.\nMTN, 9Mobile, Airtel (Zain) and GLO are all available service providers.\nGo next.\nFrom Abiriba, one can get to the connecting towns of Ohafia, Bende, and then proceed to Umuahia "}
